// U1 L2 â€” 30-question pool; 5 randomized


::U1L2-01::Which is an internal influence? {=Personal values ~Weather ~School schedule ~Advertising}

::U1L2-02::Which is an external influence? {=Peer invitation ~Personal belief ~Motivation ~Attitude}

::U1L2-03::What is a consequence? {=A possible result of a choice ~A value held by a person ~A source of heredity ~A guaranteed punishment}

::U1L2-04::A short-term consequence is one that {=may occur soon after a choice ~must last for years ~is always harmful ~cannot be predicted}

::U1L2-05::A resource is best defined as {=reliable information, a service, tool, or person that can help ~any popular opinion ~a guaranteed solution ~an internal value}

::U1L2-06::Which statement about peer influence is most accurate? {=It can support healthy or unhealthy choices ~It is always negative ~It completely controls decisions ~It is an internal influence}

::U1L2-07::Which statement about heredity is accurate? {=It may affect risk without determining every outcome ~It guarantees a health outcome ~It is a short-term consequence ~It can always be changed immediately}

::U1L2-08::What is the first MLA decision-making step? {=Identify the decision ~Choose an option ~Evaluate the outcome ~Ask a peer}

::U1L2-09::Why identify relevant facts before choosing? {=To separate evidence from assumptions ~To guarantee success ~To eliminate all disadvantages ~To avoid considering influences}

::U1L2-10::A responsible options list should {=include several realistic alternatives ~include only yes and no ~exclude modification ~begin with a predetermined answer}

::U1L2-11::A student values rest but receives late-night messages. The value placed on rest is {=an internal influence ~an external influence ~a long-term consequence ~a resource}

::U1L2-12::A safe recreation space closes at sunset. The closing time is {=an external environmental influence ~an internal belief ~a protective attitude ~heredity}

::U1L2-13::Which is the strongest evidence-based sentence? {="Because the route lacks lighting, choosing a different route may reduce risk." ~"The route will definitely cause an injury." ~"Anyone using the route is irresponsible." ~"Motivation makes the lighting unimportant."}

::U1L2-14::Which decision most clearly calls for collaboration? {=A choice affects shared transportation and requires verified schedule information ~Choosing between two pencils ~Selecting a notebook heading ~Deciding which paragraph to reread}

::U1L2-15::Why can asking for help be responsible? {=It can provide missing facts or support when risk exceeds ordinary self-management ~It transfers all responsibility ~It guarantees the preferred outcome ~It removes the need to reason}

::U1L2-16::A choice provides immediate enjoyment but repeatedly interferes with sleep. Strong analysis should {=compare the immediate benefit with possible later concentration effects ~ignore the immediate benefit ~claim the choice always causes failure ~use appearance as evidence}

::U1L2-17::Which option best shows modification? {=Changing the time or setting to reduce risk while preserving the goal ~Ignoring the risk ~Abandoning every goal permanently ~Assuming risk does not apply}

::U1L2-18::When outcomes differ among people, a careful prediction should use {=may or could ~will always ~must ~proves}

::U1L2-19::Which is a protective factor in a decision? {=Access to a trusted adult with reliable information ~Missing transportation information ~Unsafe equipment ~Pressure to hide a risk}

::U1L2-20::Which is a risk factor? {=Poor lighting on a planned route ~A verified safe alternative ~A clear emergency plan ~Accurate schedule information}

::U1L2-21::A student has incomplete menu information and a shared household budget. The strongest next step is to {=review available menus and collaborate on realistic choices ~invent missing details ~ignore cost ~assume there is only one option}

::U1L2-22::Which option is most health-supporting? {=The safest realistic option supported by current facts ~The most popular option regardless of risk ~The option with no immediate effort ~The option another person always chooses}

::U1L2-23::Why evaluate an outcome? {=To determine whether the choice worked and needs adjustment ~To prove the original choice was perfect ~To eliminate future decision-making ~To change the facts}

::U1L2-24::A family tradition affects meal choices. This is primarily {=an external cultural influence ~an internal attitude only ~a short-term consequence ~an evaluation method}

::U1L2-25::Which statement distinguishes influence from control? {=An influence can shape pressure or opportunity without forcing one outcome ~Every influence forces a choice ~Only internal factors influence decisions ~External factors can always be ignored}

::U1L2-26::Two options both have disadvantages. A responsible decision-maker should {=compare tradeoffs and choose the safer, more health-supporting option ~claim neither is possible ~choose randomly ~ignore long-term effects}

::U1L2-27::Which conclusion goes beyond the evidence? {="Because Jordan stayed up once, Jordan has a serious disorder." ~"The late night may contribute to tiredness." ~"A planned stopping time may reduce risk." ~"A timer is an available resource."}

::U1L2-28::A friend suggests a healthy routine. This demonstrates that peer influence {=can be protective ~is always harmful ~is heredity ~eliminates personal choice}

::U1L2-29::Which sequence correctly represents the core process? {=facts â†’ influences â†’ options â†’ consequences â†’ choice â†’ evaluation ~choice â†’ assumptions â†’ facts â†’ ignore outcome ~consequences â†’ heredity â†’ no options ~peers â†’ control â†’ guaranteed result}

::U1L2-30::Which final justification is strongest? {="I chose the adjusted plan because it uses the verified resource, reduces the stated risk, and still supports the goal." ~"I chose it because it feels right." ~"I chose it because everyone must agree." ~"I chose it because no disadvantage is possible."}

